          "heading": "The system before the material label",
          "content": "**Guiding question:** What does a sustainable material actually deliver, and over what useful lifetime?\n\nA university specifies a new fleet of classroom furniture. Procurement can minimize kilograms, initial carbon, purchase price, maintenance downtime, or replaced chairs, but these objectives do not rank products identically. A product comparison only becomes meaningful after its required service, physical boundaries, time and mandatory safety specifications are written down. The system may contain long-lived inventories, poorly measured return streams, process losses, upstream impacts and financial constraints. The engineering objective is to find the particular intervention that actually improves useful output per resource and reduces meaningful harms. The unit combines an explicit material mechanism with a data and decision workflow, rather than presenting recycling, repair, substitution or efficiency as universally superior.\n\nAn evidence-ready project in this unit starts with **service reliability and traceable stock ledger**. Its indispensable records include **count useful delivered services, batch identity, retained stock, exits, returns and actual product failures**, and important counterevidence comes from **customer cohort tracking paired with physical mass records**. Before reading any calculated result, consider **whether added efficiency increases use volume enough to increase total production**. If the answer can be changed by a reasonable assumption about material quality or service life, the proper result is a bounded comparison and a plan to collect missing evidence, not a confident environmental winner.",
