          "secNumber": "1.3",
          "heading": "Lesson 1.2 \u2014 Circular stocks and flow mapping",
          "content": "### 1. Physics, chemistry and the function actually supplied\n\nStocks persist in buildings, vehicles and equipment while material flows enter, circulate and leave those stocks. A system can show high collection rates yet have little closed-loop material return because of losses, exports or accumulation in long-lived assets. Flow accounting requires explicit boundaries and consistent mass bases, including moisture, alloy constituents and products with multiple materials. Marketed recycling rates need a specified denominator: generated, collected, processed, or finally recovered output.\n\n**Working relationship:** Input + opening stock = output + closing stock + accounted losses on a matching time basis. Keep the system boundary, physical units and quality specification visible before asserting an avoided impact.\n\n### 2. A real engineering dilemma with rival explanations\n\n**Problem statement:** A municipality celebrates 90% separate collection of aluminum beverage packaging; a smelter reports much lower qualified metal return. Trace every intermediate mass. Propose two rival mechanisms and one observation that differentiates them. Record timing, composition and functional performance before ranking either explanation.\n\n**Constraint that can reverse the answer:** Informal collection, stockpiles, measurement moisture and discarded process rejects can make apparent recovery greater than reality. Estimate which service, quality or environmental condition could reverse the conclusion.\n\n### 3. Transparent synthetic calculation and sensitivity\n\nThe paired interactive card for this lesson is **Material stock under fixed flows**. Its computational expression is `5000+140*y`, with adjustable **Years of steady net accumulation** in years. At 6 years the deterministic output is **5840 tonnes**; at 16 years it is **7240 tonnes**. The two-point sensitivity is 140 tonnes per years. These are synthetic teaching calculations, not measurements. Independently verify units and a third input before interpretation.\n\n**Why the numerical output has limits:** Start stock 5000 t; inflow 400 t/y; outflow 260 t/y; no nonlinear demand or stock aging. For a real decision, independently measure the variables that this illustrative formula holds constant.\n\n### 4. Evidence protocol, sampling and falsification\n\n**Specific investigation:** Audit weighbridge records, contamination samples and final saleable specification, reconciling independent reporting points. Define independent samples, record rejected outputs and capture instrument calibration and field-relevant service conditions.\n\n Verify both mass-balance closure and functionally qualified output; publish observations that contradict the preferred mechanism.\n\n### 5. Design trade-off and conditions of adoption\n\n**Trade-off:** Increasing collection without processing capacity may cause storage or downcycling rather than circular substitution. Evaluate the trade-off only after minimum safety, quality and legal requirements are satisfied.\n\n### 6. Three guided practice problems\n\n**Question A \u2014 reconstruct the physical account.** Starting from \u201cInput + opening stock = output + closing stock + accounted losses on a matching time basis.\u201d, identify the relevant system, quantities, physical units and hidden assumptions. Draw material or energy arrows, label the conserved quantity and explain at least two potential losses or quality changes. **Solution guidance:** reconcile consistent physical units and include process losses.\n\n**Question B \u2014 independent worked sensitivity.** Recalculate 5840 tonnes from an input of 6 years; calculate a third point midway between 6 and 16, using the formula rather than copying the interactive output. Explain why a difference of 1400 tonnes is not automatically a change in environmental benefit or service economics. **Solution guidance:** numerical sensitivity does not validate a site-specific benefit.\n\n**Question C \u2014 design a decisive controlled comparison.** For \u201cA municipality celebrates 90% separate collection of aluminum beverage packaging; a smelter reports much lower qualified metal return. Trace every intermediate mass.\u201d, provide a sample plan, at least one suitable control and one evidence source that does not share the same central assumption as the model. Describe the plausible observation that would invalidate the preferred conclusion. **Solution guidance:** compare equivalent service and independently qualified outputs.\n\n### 7. Decision memo and reader challenge\n\nWrite a memo that separates observations from synthetic assumptions and proposes a reversible pilot with explicit stop criteria. Increasing collection without processing capacity may cause storage or downcycling rather than circular substitution.\n\n<!-- MODEL_0 -->",
