          "content": "### 1. Physics, chemistry and the function actually supplied\n\nReducing kilograms per product can reduce extraction but may also change durability, maintenance or energy use. Material intensity should be tied to the unit of service and to the life-cycle stages affected by redesign. Substitution has cascading implications: thin sheet can demand new coatings, laminated joints can prevent disassembly, or lightweight vehicles can have manufacturing penalties offset by operating savings. A sensitivity analysis should show the break-even utilization at which redesign changes net impacts.\n\n**Working relationship:** Total burden = initial manufacture + cumulative operation + replacements + end-of-life over the defined service horizon. Keep the system boundary, physical units and quality specification visible before asserting an avoided impact.\n\n### 2. A real engineering dilemma with rival explanations\n\n**Problem statement:** An engineering team replaces a steel housing with a polymer composite that halves mass but complicates high-value recovery. Propose two rival mechanisms and one observation that differentiates them. Record timing, composition and functional performance before ranking either explanation.\n\n**Constraint that can reverse the answer:** Mass alone is not an environmental impact metric; low-mass, high-impact production and difficult disposal may reverse rankings. Estimate which service, quality or environmental condition could reverse the conclusion.\n\n### 3. Transparent synthetic calculation and sensitivity\n\nThe paired interactive card for this lesson is **Burden per achieved service**. Its computational expression is `150/n+3`, with adjustable **Achieved uses per product** in uses. At 15 uses the deterministic output is **13 kg CO\u2082e/use**; at 35 uses it is **7.2857143 kg CO\u2082e/use**. The two-point sensitivity is -0.28571429 kg CO\u2082e/use per uses. These are synthetic teaching calculations, not measurements. Independently verify units and a third input before interpretation.\n\n**Why the numerical output has limits:** Synthetic manufacture 150 kg CO\u2082e/product, fixed use burden 3 kg CO\u2082e/use, no returns or failure effects. For a real decision, independently measure the variables that this illustrative formula holds constant.\n\n### 4. Evidence protocol, sampling and falsification\n\n**Specific investigation:** Measure representative energy consumption, repairs and actual end-of-life pathways for both designs. Define independent samples, record rejected outputs and capture instrument calibration and field-relevant service conditions.\n\n Verify both mass-balance closure and functionally qualified output; publish observations that contradict the preferred mechanism.\n\n### 5. Design trade-off and conditions of adoption\n\n**Trade-off:** A low-mass option can be desirable for mobility but not for a stationary product where material separability dominates. Evaluate the trade-off only after minimum safety, quality and legal requirements are satisfied.\n\n### 6. Three guided practice problems\n\n**Question A \u2014 reconstruct the physical account.** Starting from \u201cTotal burden = initial manufacture + cumulative operation + replacements + end-of-life over the defined service horizon.\u201d, identify the relevant system, quantities, physical units and hidden assumptions. Draw material or energy arrows, label the conserved quantity and explain at least two potential losses or quality changes. **Solution guidance:** reconcile consistent physical units and include process losses.\n\n**Question B \u2014 independent worked sensitivity.** Recalculate 13 kg CO\u2082e/use from an input of 15 uses; calculate a third point midway between 15 and 35, using the formula rather than copying the interactive output. Explain why a difference of -5.7142857 kg CO\u2082e/use is not automatically a change in environmental benefit or service economics. **Solution guidance:** numerical sensitivity does not validate a site-specific benefit.\n\n**Question C \u2014 design a decisive controlled comparison.** For \u201cAn engineering team replaces a steel housing with a polymer composite that halves mass but complicates high-value recovery.\u201d, provide a sample plan, at least one suitable control and one evidence source that does not share the same central assumption as the model. Describe the plausible observation that would invalidate the preferred conclusion. **Solution guidance:** compare equivalent service and independently qualified outputs.\n\n### 7. Decision memo and reader challenge\n\nWrite a memo that separates observations from synthetic assumptions and proposes a reversible pilot with explicit stop criteria. A low-mass option can be desirable for mobility but not for a stationary product where material separability dominates.",
