          "heading": "Lesson 2.1 \u2014 LCA goal, scope and system boundaries",
          "content": "### 1. Physics, chemistry and the function actually supplied\n\nA life-cycle assessment starts by stating why a comparison is made, its audience and whether results will support a public comparative assertion. The functional unit, reference flows, geographical and temporal coverage, cut-off rules and impact categories should be decided before the desired outcome is known. Cradle-to-gate and cradle-to-grave results answer different questions. Including or excluding maintenance and end-of-life can reverse a ranking even if manufacturing inventories are identical.\n\n**Working relationship:** Life-cycle impact per functional unit is the sum of inventory flows multiplied by category-specific characterization factors, subject to defined methods. Keep the system boundary, physical units and quality specification visible before asserting an avoided impact.\n\n### 2. A real engineering dilemma with rival explanations\n\n**Problem statement:** Two flooring types show opposite rankings depending on whether seven years of replacement are included. Prepare a transparent comparison protocol. Propose two rival mechanisms and one observation that differentiates them. Record timing, composition and functional performance before ranking either explanation.\n\n**Constraint that can reverse the answer:** A truncated boundary can hide electricity, transport, installation or replacement burdens; impact categories are not interchangeable. Estimate which service, quality or environmental condition could reverse the conclusion.\n\n### 3. Transparent synthetic calculation and sensitivity\n\nThe paired interactive card for this lesson is **Manufacturing plus transport inventory**. Its computational expression is `50+0.08*d`, with adjustable **Delivery distance for one tonne** in km. At 200 km the deterministic output is **66 kg CO\u2082e**; at 620 km it is **99.6 kg CO\u2082e**. The two-point sensitivity is 0.08 kg CO\u2082e per km. These are synthetic teaching calculations, not measurements. Independently verify units and a third input before interpretation.\n\n**Why the numerical output has limits:** One-tonne output, 50 kg CO\u2082e base and 0.08 kg CO\u2082e/t-km transportation factor; no route/load complications. For a real decision, independently measure the variables that this illustrative formula holds constant.\n\n### 4. Evidence protocol, sampling and falsification\n\n**Specific investigation:** Write a registered scope sheet and challenge each cut-off with a high-impact sensitivity case. Define independent samples, record rejected outputs and capture instrument calibration and field-relevant service conditions.\n\n Verify both mass-balance closure and functionally qualified output; publish observations that contradict the preferred mechanism.\n\n### 5. Design trade-off and conditions of adoption\n\n**Trade-off:** A low cradle-to-gate footprint may be outweighed by energy, cleaning or premature replacement during use. Evaluate the trade-off only after minimum safety, quality and legal requirements are satisfied.\n\n### 6. Three guided practice problems\n\n**Question A \u2014 reconstruct the physical account.** Starting from \u201cLife-cycle impact per functional unit is the sum of inventory flows multiplied by category-specific characterization factors, subject to defined methods.\u201d, identify the relevant system, quantities, physical units and hidden assumptions. Draw material or energy arrows, label the conserved quantity and explain at least two potential losses or quality changes. **Solution guidance:** reconcile consistent physical units and include process losses.\n\n**Question B \u2014 independent worked sensitivity.** Recalculate 66 kg CO\u2082e from an input of 200 km; calculate a third point midway between 200 and 620, using the formula rather than copying the interactive output. Explain why a difference of 33.6 kg CO\u2082e is not automatically a change in environmental benefit or service economics. **Solution guidance:** numerical sensitivity does not validate a site-specific benefit.\n\n**Question C \u2014 design a decisive controlled comparison.** For \u201cTwo flooring types show opposite rankings depending on whether seven years of replacement are included. Prepare a transparent comparison protocol.\u201d, provide a sample plan, at least one suitable control and one evidence source that does not share the same central assumption as the model. Describe the plausible observation that would invalidate the preferred conclusion. **Solution guidance:** compare equivalent service and independently qualified outputs.\n\n### 7. Decision memo and reader challenge\n\nWrite a memo that separates observations from synthetic assumptions and proposes a reversible pilot with explicit stop criteria. A low cradle-to-gate footprint may be outweighed by energy, cleaning or premature replacement during use.",
