          "secNumber": "2.4",
          "heading": "Lesson 2.2 \u2014 Inventory data, provenance and representativeness",
          "content": "### 1. Physics, chemistry and the function actually supplied\n\nLife-cycle inventory links measured operations, purchased inputs, transport modes and waste outcomes to a reproducible flow table. Primary process data are preferable when available, but secondary regional databases can fill gaps only with their uncertainty made visible. Grid mixes, recycled material allocations, furnace technologies and production years change environmental intensity. A data source that is geographically convenient may be technically wrong for an individual supplier.\n\n**Working relationship:** Impact = \u03a3(activity quantity \u00d7 emission or impact factor); every factor needs compatible units and traceable provenance. Keep the system boundary, physical units and quality specification visible before asserting an avoided impact.\n\n### 2. A real engineering dilemma with rival explanations\n\n**Problem statement:** A factory averages production electricity for a product whose high-temperature step operates mainly at night. Decide the significance. Propose two rival mechanisms and one observation that differentiates them. Record timing, composition and functional performance before ranking either explanation.\n\n**Constraint that can reverse the answer:** Using an obsolete grid factor or mismatched steelmaking route can dominate precision gained by more decimal places. Estimate which service, quality or environmental condition could reverse the conclusion.\n\n### 3. Transparent synthetic calculation and sensitivity\n\nThe paired interactive card for this lesson is **Attributional burden allocation**. Its computational expression is `500*p/100`, with adjustable **Co-product allocation share** in %. At 24 % the deterministic output is **120 kg CO\u2082e**; at 66 % it is **330 kg CO\u2082e**. The two-point sensitivity is 5 kg CO\u2082e per %. These are synthetic teaching calculations, not measurements. Independently verify units and a third input before interpretation.\n\n**Why the numerical output has limits:** Synthetic 500 kg CO\u2082e joint-process burden; shares sum to 100%; allocation is a convention, not a physical observed effect. For a real decision, independently measure the variables that this illustrative formula holds constant.\n\n### 4. Evidence protocol, sampling and falsification\n\n**Specific investigation:** Collect bills, process logs and supplier data and vary the highest-contributing uncertain inputs. Define independent samples, record rejected outputs and capture instrument calibration and field-relevant service conditions.\n\n Verify both mass-balance closure and functionally qualified output; publish observations that contradict the preferred mechanism.\n\n### 5. Design trade-off and conditions of adoption\n\n**Trade-off:** More detailed models can become less reliable if detailed-looking estimates disguise low-quality underlying data. Evaluate the trade-off only after minimum safety, quality and legal requirements are satisfied.\n\n### 6. Three guided practice problems\n\n**Question A \u2014 reconstruct the physical account.** Starting from \u201cImpact = \u03a3(activity quantity \u00d7 emission or impact factor); every factor needs compatible units and traceable provenance.\u201d, identify the relevant system, quantities, physical units and hidden assumptions. Draw material or energy arrows, label the conserved quantity and explain at least two potential losses or quality changes. **Solution guidance:** reconcile consistent physical units and include process losses.\n\n**Question B \u2014 independent worked sensitivity.** Recalculate 120 kg CO\u2082e from an input of 24 %; calculate a third point midway between 24 and 66, using the formula rather than copying the interactive output. Explain why a difference of 210 kg CO\u2082e is not automatically a change in environmental benefit or service economics. **Solution guidance:** numerical sensitivity does not validate a site-specific benefit.\n\n**Question C \u2014 design a decisive controlled comparison.** For \u201cA factory averages production electricity for a product whose high-temperature step operates mainly at night. Decide the significance.\u201d, provide a sample plan, at least one suitable control and one evidence source that does not share the same central assumption as the model. Describe the plausible observation that would invalidate the preferred conclusion. **Solution guidance:** compare equivalent service and independently qualified outputs.\n\n### 7. Decision memo and reader challenge\n\nWrite a memo that separates observations from synthetic assumptions and proposes a reversible pilot with explicit stop criteria. More detailed models can become less reliable if detailed-looking estimates disguise low-quality underlying data.\n\n<!-- MODEL_2 -->",
