          "heading": "Lesson 2.3 \u2014 Impact categories and trade-off interpretation",
          "content": "### 1. Physics, chemistry and the function actually supplied\n\nClimate change expressed in carbon-dioxide equivalents is only one impact category. Eutrophication, acidification, toxicity, freshwater use, land use and resource-related indicators can lead to different rankings. Characterization models carry uncertainties and assumptions; results should not be arbitrarily added together unless normalization or weighting choices are explicit. An increase in electricity for advanced separation can lower toxic releases or improve material recovery while increasing climate impacts under a high-carbon grid.\n\n**Working relationship:** For category k, I_k = \u03a3_j Q_j CF_(j,k) with explicit flow quantities and characterization factors. Keep the system boundary, physical units and quality specification visible before asserting an avoided impact.\n\n### 2. A real engineering dilemma with rival explanations\n\n**Problem statement:** A recycling solvent eliminates contamination but raises water use. Assess climate, water stress, worker safety and local wastewater constraints. Propose two rival mechanisms and one observation that differentiates them. Record timing, composition and functional performance before ranking either explanation.\n\n**Constraint that can reverse the answer:** A single weighted environmental score can hide unacceptable local harms or uncertain toxicity factors. Estimate which service, quality or environmental condition could reverse the conclusion.\n\n### 3. Transparent synthetic calculation and sensitivity\n\nThe paired interactive card for this lesson is **Manufacturing plus transport inventory**. Its computational expression is `50+0.08*d`, with adjustable **Delivery distance for one tonne** in km. At 280 km the deterministic output is **72.4 kg CO\u2082e**; at 690 km it is **105.2 kg CO\u2082e**. The two-point sensitivity is 0.08 kg CO\u2082e per km. These are synthetic teaching calculations, not measurements. Independently verify units and a third input before interpretation.\n\n**Why the numerical output has limits:** One-tonne output, 50 kg CO\u2082e base and 0.08 kg CO\u2082e/t-km transportation factor; no route/load complications. For a real decision, independently measure the variables that this illustrative formula holds constant.\n\n### 4. Evidence protocol, sampling and falsification\n\n**Specific investigation:** Retain unaggregated category indicators and test whether trade-offs persist across alternative methods. Define independent samples, record rejected outputs and capture instrument calibration and field-relevant service conditions.\n\n Verify both mass-balance closure and functionally qualified output; publish observations that contradict the preferred mechanism.\n\n### 5. Design trade-off and conditions of adoption\n\n**Trade-off:** Improving global climate results is not sufficient evidence that a local community experiences lower pollution. Evaluate the trade-off only after minimum safety, quality and legal requirements are satisfied.\n\n### 6. Three guided practice problems\n\n**Question A \u2014 reconstruct the physical account.** Starting from \u201cFor category k, I_k = \u03a3_j Q_j CF_(j,k) with explicit flow quantities and characterization factors.\u201d, identify the relevant system, quantities, physical units and hidden assumptions. Draw material or energy arrows, label the conserved quantity and explain at least two potential losses or quality changes. **Solution guidance:** reconcile consistent physical units and include process losses.\n\n**Question B \u2014 independent worked sensitivity.** Recalculate 72.4 kg CO\u2082e from an input of 280 km; calculate a third point midway between 280 and 690, using the formula rather than copying the interactive output. Explain why a difference of 32.8 kg CO\u2082e is not automatically a change in environmental benefit or service economics. **Solution guidance:** numerical sensitivity does not validate a site-specific benefit.\n\n**Question C \u2014 design a decisive controlled comparison.** For \u201cA recycling solvent eliminates contamination but raises water use. Assess climate, water stress, worker safety and local wastewater constraints.\u201d, provide a sample plan, at least one suitable control and one evidence source that does not share the same central assumption as the model. Describe the plausible observation that would invalidate the preferred conclusion. **Solution guidance:** compare equivalent service and independently qualified outputs.\n\n### 7. Decision memo and reader challenge\n\nWrite a memo that separates observations from synthetic assumptions and proposes a reversible pilot with explicit stop criteria. Improving global climate results is not sufficient evidence that a local community experiences lower pollution.",
