          "heading": "Lesson 2.4 \u2014 Allocation, co-products and recycled content",
          "content": "### 1. Physics, chemistry and the function actually supplied\n\nManufacturing operations often produce several saleable products or recovered streams. Attributional models require allocation or system partition rules when processes cannot be subdivided. Mass and economic allocation distribute burdens differently. Recycled-content and end-of-life approaches can also double count credit if both parties claim the same avoided virgin production. Circular systems should report allocation assumptions and avoid presenting a methodological convention as an observed physical fact.\n\n**Working relationship:** Allocated burden_i = total joint burden \u00d7 allocation share_i, with all shares summing to one. Keep the system boundary, physical units and quality specification visible before asserting an avoided impact.\n\n### 2. A real engineering dilemma with rival explanations\n\n**Problem statement:** A copper smelter produces cathode copper, precious-metal byproducts and sulfuric acid. Two studies use incompatible burden allocations. Propose two rival mechanisms and one observation that differentiates them. Record timing, composition and functional performance before ranking either explanation.\n\n**Constraint that can reverse the answer:** Market price volatility can change economic allocation while physics stays constant. Estimate which service, quality or environmental condition could reverse the conclusion.\n\n### 3. Transparent synthetic calculation and sensitivity\n\nThe paired interactive card for this lesson is **Attributional burden allocation**. Its computational expression is `500*p/100`, with adjustable **Co-product allocation share** in %. At 32 % the deterministic output is **160 kg CO\u2082e**; at 72 % it is **360 kg CO\u2082e**. The two-point sensitivity is 5 kg CO\u2082e per %. These are synthetic teaching calculations, not measurements. Independently verify units and a third input before interpretation.\n\n**Why the numerical output has limits:** Synthetic 500 kg CO\u2082e joint-process burden; shares sum to 100%; allocation is a convention, not a physical observed effect. For a real decision, independently measure the variables that this illustrative formula holds constant.\n\n### 4. Evidence protocol, sampling and falsification\n\n**Specific investigation:** Publish total process inventory and compare at least two transparent allocation conventions. Define independent samples, record rejected outputs and capture instrument calibration and field-relevant service conditions.\n\n Verify both mass-balance closure and functionally qualified output; publish observations that contradict the preferred mechanism.\n\n### 5. Design trade-off and conditions of adoption\n\n**Trade-off:** Allocation that rewards recovery on paper does not automatically correspond to real additional displacement in the market. Evaluate the trade-off only after minimum safety, quality and legal requirements are satisfied.\n\n### 6. Three guided practice problems\n\n**Question A \u2014 reconstruct the physical account.** Starting from \u201cAllocated burden_i = total joint burden \u00d7 allocation share_i, with all shares summing to one.\u201d, identify the relevant system, quantities, physical units and hidden assumptions. Draw material or energy arrows, label the conserved quantity and explain at least two potential losses or quality changes. **Solution guidance:** reconcile consistent physical units and include process losses.\n\n**Question B \u2014 independent worked sensitivity.** Recalculate 160 kg CO\u2082e from an input of 32 %; calculate a third point midway between 32 and 72, using the formula rather than copying the interactive output. Explain why a difference of 200 kg CO\u2082e is not automatically a change in environmental benefit or service economics. **Solution guidance:** numerical sensitivity does not validate a site-specific benefit.\n\n**Question C \u2014 design a decisive controlled comparison.** For \u201cA copper smelter produces cathode copper, precious-metal byproducts and sulfuric acid. Two studies use incompatible burden allocations.\u201d, provide a sample plan, at least one suitable control and one evidence source that does not share the same central assumption as the model. Describe the plausible observation that would invalidate the preferred conclusion. **Solution guidance:** compare equivalent service and independently qualified outputs.\n\n### 7. Decision memo and reader challenge\n\nWrite a memo that separates observations from synthetic assumptions and proposes a reversible pilot with explicit stop criteria. Allocation that rewards recovery on paper does not automatically correspond to real additional displacement in the market.\n\n<!-- MODEL_3 -->",
