          "heading": "Lesson 2.5 \u2014 Uncertainty, scenarios and review",
          "content": "### 1. Physics, chemistry and the function actually supplied\n\nParameter uncertainty includes measurement error and variation in process conditions; model uncertainty includes boundary choices and assumed future recovery pathways. Monte Carlo sampling quantifies only the uncertainty encoded in its inputs and dependence structure. Correlated electricity intensity and operating temperature cannot be treated as unrelated if they move together. Review should separately check data traceability, arithmetic, scope consistency and whether conclusions exceed the evidence.\n\n**Working relationship:** For independent first-order inputs, \u03c3_y\u00b2 \u2248 \u03a3(\u2202y/\u2202x_i)\u00b2\u03c3_i\u00b2; correlations add covariance terms. Keep the system boundary, physical units and quality specification visible before asserting an avoided impact.\n\n### 2. A real engineering dilemma with rival explanations\n\n**Problem statement:** A published footprint has a narrow interval because transport and end-of-life were treated as exact despite missing records. Propose two rival mechanisms and one observation that differentiates them. Record timing, composition and functional performance before ranking either explanation.\n\n**Constraint that can reverse the answer:** Precise software outputs cannot rescue biased or unrepresentative source inventories. Estimate which service, quality or environmental condition could reverse the conclusion.\n\n### 3. Transparent synthetic calculation and sensitivity\n\nThe paired interactive card for this lesson is **Manufacturing plus transport inventory**. Its computational expression is `50+0.08*d`, with adjustable **Delivery distance for one tonne** in km. At 360 km the deterministic output is **78.8 kg CO\u2082e**; at 760 km it is **110.8 kg CO\u2082e**. The two-point sensitivity is 0.08 kg CO\u2082e per km. These are synthetic teaching calculations, not measurements. Independently verify units and a third input before interpretation.\n\n**Why the numerical output has limits:** One-tonne output, 50 kg CO\u2082e base and 0.08 kg CO\u2082e/t-km transportation factor; no route/load complications. For a real decision, independently measure the variables that this illustrative formula holds constant.\n\n### 4. Evidence protocol, sampling and falsification\n\n**Specific investigation:** Recompute baseline and high/low credible cases, document exclusions and seek independent methodological review. Define independent samples, record rejected outputs and capture instrument calibration and field-relevant service conditions.\n\n Verify both mass-balance closure and functionally qualified output; publish observations that contradict the preferred mechanism.\n\n### 5. Design trade-off and conditions of adoption\n\n**Trade-off:** The least uncertain option numerically may not be the least risky if uncertainty was suppressed by assumption. Evaluate the trade-off only after minimum safety, quality and legal requirements are satisfied.\n\n### 6. Three guided practice problems\n\n**Question A \u2014 reconstruct the physical account.** Starting from \u201cFor independent first-order inputs, \u03c3_y\u00b2 \u2248 \u03a3(\u2202y/\u2202x_i)\u00b2\u03c3_i\u00b2; correlations add covariance terms.\u201d, identify the relevant system, quantities, physical units and hidden assumptions. Draw material or energy arrows, label the conserved quantity and explain at least two potential losses or quality changes. **Solution guidance:** reconcile consistent physical units and include process losses.\n\n**Question B \u2014 independent worked sensitivity.** Recalculate 78.8 kg CO\u2082e from an input of 360 km; calculate a third point midway between 360 and 760, using the formula rather than copying the interactive output. Explain why a difference of 32 kg CO\u2082e is not automatically a change in environmental benefit or service economics. **Solution guidance:** numerical sensitivity does not validate a site-specific benefit.\n\n**Question C \u2014 design a decisive controlled comparison.** For \u201cA published footprint has a narrow interval because transport and end-of-life were treated as exact despite missing records.\u201d, provide a sample plan, at least one suitable control and one evidence source that does not share the same central assumption as the model. Describe the plausible observation that would invalidate the preferred conclusion. **Solution guidance:** compare equivalent service and independently qualified outputs.\n\n### 7. Decision memo and reader challenge\n\nWrite a memo that separates observations from synthetic assumptions and proposes a reversible pilot with explicit stop criteria. The least uncertain option numerically may not be the least risky if uncertainty was suppressed by assumption.",
