          "secNumber": "3.1",
          "heading": "The system before the material label",
          "content": "**Guiding question:** How do alloy chemistry, furnace operation and scrap composition govern secondary-metal value?\n\nA foundry plans to raise post-consumer scrap fraction while continuing to promise high-ductility automotive components. Scrap contamination and energy demand interact with melt yield and quality losses. A product comparison only becomes meaningful after its required service, physical boundaries, time and mandatory safety specifications are written down. The system may contain long-lived inventories, poorly measured return streams, process losses, upstream impacts and financial constraints. The engineering objective is to find the particular intervention that actually improves useful output per resource and reduces meaningful harms. The unit combines an explicit material mechanism with a data and decision workflow, rather than presenting recycling, repair, substitution or efficiency as universally superior.\n\nAn evidence-ready project in this unit starts with **chemistry-controlled feed and qualified remelt output**. Its indispensable records include **record elemental assays, dross/slags, energy meter, batch yield and downstream acceptance**, and important counterevidence comes from **paired feedstock lots and reference-grade manufacturing controls**. Before reading any calculated result, consider **how much primary feed or sorting is required to meet every specification**. If the answer can be changed by a reasonable assumption about material quality or service life, the proper result is a bounded comparison and a plan to collect missing evidence, not a confident environmental winner.",
