          "content": "### 1. Physics, chemistry and the function actually supplied\n\nPrimary ironmaking commonly reduces iron oxides using carbon-bearing reductants, while scrap-based electric furnaces remelt metallic feed. The emissions and resource profiles depend on actual energy supply, reductants, scrap preparation, alloy grades and downstream operations. Recycled content does not erase furnaces, oxygen production, electrode consumption, slag handling or yield loss. Grade-matched clean scrap can support high-quality steelmaking; mixed scrap may require dilution with lower-contaminant iron.\n\n**Working relationship:** Saleable steel = charged metal \u00d7 melt-to-product yield, under a defined chemistry and quality specification. Keep the system boundary, physical units and quality specification visible before asserting an avoided impact.\n\n### 2. A real engineering dilemma with rival explanations\n\n**Problem statement:** An automotive sheet supplier proposes 100% mixed scrap. Evaluate tin, copper and residual limits before promising deep-drawing performance. Propose two rival mechanisms and one observation that differentiates them. Record timing, composition and functional performance before ranking either explanation.\n\n**Constraint that can reverse the answer:** A carbon comparison based only on furnace labels hides electricity mix, upstream materials and downstream finishing. Estimate which service, quality or environmental condition could reverse the conclusion.\n\n### 3. Transparent synthetic calculation and sensitivity\n\nThe paired interactive card for this lesson is **Ideal dilution of scrap impurity**. Its computational expression is `(0.5*(100-p)+0.02*p)/100`, with adjustable **Clean-metal blend share** in %. At 20 % the deterministic output is **0.404 wt% Cu**; at 62 % it is **0.2024 wt% Cu**. The two-point sensitivity is -0.0048 wt% Cu per %. These are synthetic teaching calculations, not measurements. Independently verify units and a third input before interpretation.\n\n**Why the numerical output has limits:** Dirty scrap contains 0.50 wt% copper, clean metal 0.02 wt%; perfect mixing and no elemental loss. For a real decision, independently measure the variables that this illustrative formula holds constant.\n\n### 4. Evidence protocol, sampling and falsification\n\n**Specific investigation:** Sample heats for trace chemistry and mechanical performance; audit electricity and yield data for each route. Define independent samples, record rejected outputs and capture instrument calibration and field-relevant service conditions.\n\n Verify both mass-balance closure and functionally qualified output; publish observations that contradict the preferred mechanism.\n\n### 5. Design trade-off and conditions of adoption\n\n**Trade-off:** Maximizing nominal scrap charge can increase rejects or primary-metal dilution if residual elements exceed limits. Evaluate the trade-off only after minimum safety, quality and legal requirements are satisfied.\n\n### 6. Three guided practice problems\n\n**Question A \u2014 reconstruct the physical account.** Starting from \u201cSaleable steel = charged metal \u00d7 melt-to-product yield, under a defined chemistry and quality specification.\u201d, identify the relevant system, quantities, physical units and hidden assumptions. Draw material or energy arrows, label the conserved quantity and explain at least two potential losses or quality changes. **Solution guidance:** reconcile consistent physical units and include process losses.\n\n**Question B \u2014 independent worked sensitivity.** Recalculate 0.404 wt% Cu from an input of 20 %; calculate a third point midway between 20 and 62, using the formula rather than copying the interactive output. Explain why a difference of -0.2016 wt% Cu is not automatically a change in environmental benefit or service economics. **Solution guidance:** numerical sensitivity does not validate a site-specific benefit.\n\n**Question C \u2014 design a decisive controlled comparison.** For \u201cAn automotive sheet supplier proposes 100% mixed scrap. Evaluate tin, copper and residual limits before promising deep-drawing performance.\u201d, provide a sample plan, at least one suitable control and one evidence source that does not share the same central assumption as the model. Describe the plausible observation that would invalidate the preferred conclusion. **Solution guidance:** compare equivalent service and independently qualified outputs.\n\n### 7. Decision memo and reader challenge\n\nWrite a memo that separates observations from synthetic assumptions and proposes a reversible pilot with explicit stop criteria. Maximizing nominal scrap charge can increase rejects or primary-metal dilution if residual elements exceed limits.",
