          "heading": "The system before the material label",
          "content": "**Guiding question:** How should long-lived buildings balance durability, embodied impacts and end-of-life recovery?\n\nA city compares new concrete, reused steel, recovered aggregates and modular envelopes in buildings with uncertain future renovation dates and thermal performance. A product comparison only becomes meaningful after its required service, physical boundaries, time and mandatory safety specifications are written down. The system may contain long-lived inventories, poorly measured return streams, process losses, upstream impacts and financial constraints. The engineering objective is to find the particular intervention that actually improves useful output per resource and reduces meaningful harms. The unit combines an explicit material mechanism with a data and decision workflow, rather than presenting recycling, repair, substitution or efficiency as universally superior.\n\nAn evidence-ready project in this unit starts with **equal-performing structures in a defined climate**. Its indispensable records include **collect binder chemistry, strength/durability, distance, repair record and demolition flows**, and important counterevidence comes from **structural qualification and cradle-to-grave boundary sensitivity**. Before reading any calculated result, consider **whether assumed lifespan or transport dominates projected benefits**. If the answer can be changed by a reasonable assumption about material quality or service life, the proper result is a bounded comparison and a plan to collect missing evidence, not a confident environmental winner.",
