          "heading": "Lesson 5.1 \u2014 Cement clinker substitution and chemistry",
          "content": "### 1. Physics, chemistry and the function actually supplied\n\nPortland cement emissions come from both fuel and limestone calcination. Lowering clinker fraction with suitable supplementary materials can reduce embodied impacts while changing early strength, curing, durability and supply dependencies. A substitute must be characterized for its chemistry, reactivity and long-term performance under exposure conditions. Carbon stored or captured during curing requires quantified uptake and permanence accounting; it cannot simply erase initial process emissions.\n\n**Working relationship:** Binder-related impact \u2248 clinker mass \u00d7 verified clinker factor + additive masses \u00d7 their specific factors. Keep the system boundary, physical units and quality specification visible before asserting an avoided impact.\n\n### 2. A real engineering dilemma with rival explanations\n\n**Problem statement:** A contractor specifies a low-clinker mix for marine infrastructure but lacks durability evidence for chloride exposure. Propose two rival mechanisms and one observation that differentiates them. Record timing, composition and functional performance before ranking either explanation.\n\n**Constraint that can reverse the answer:** Equal 28-day compressive strength alone does not prove equal long-term performance. Estimate which service, quality or environmental condition could reverse the conclusion.\n\n### 3. Transparent synthetic calculation and sensitivity\n\nThe paired interactive card for this lesson is **Clinker fraction sensitivity**. Its computational expression is `1000*(0.85*p/100+0.15*(1-p/100))`, with adjustable **Clinker mass fraction of binder** in %. At 36 % the deterministic output is **402 kg CO\u2082e/tonne binder**; at 70 % it is **640 kg CO\u2082e/tonne binder**. The two-point sensitivity is 7 kg CO\u2082e/tonne binder per %. These are synthetic teaching calculations, not measurements. Independently verify units and a third input before interpretation.\n\n**Why the numerical output has limits:** 1000 kg binder, hypothetical clinker factor 0.85 kg CO\u2082e/kg and substitution factor 0.15; ignores durability changes. For a real decision, independently measure the variables that this illustrative formula holds constant.\n\n### 4. Evidence protocol, sampling and falsification\n\n**Specific investigation:** Compare representative mix designs for permeability, transport, curing and field-relevant durability. Define independent samples, record rejected outputs and capture instrument calibration and field-relevant service conditions.\n\n Verify both mass-balance closure and functionally qualified output; publish observations that contradict the preferred mechanism.\n\n### 5. Design trade-off and conditions of adoption\n\n**Trade-off:** Maximizing substitution can compromise performance or depend on scarce byproducts that will decline as industries decarbonize. Evaluate the trade-off only after minimum safety, quality and legal requirements are satisfied.\n\n### 6. Three guided practice problems\n\n**Question A \u2014 reconstruct the physical account.** Starting from \u201cBinder-related impact \u2248 clinker mass \u00d7 verified clinker factor + additive masses \u00d7 their specific factors.\u201d, identify the relevant system, quantities, physical units and hidden assumptions. Draw material or energy arrows, label the conserved quantity and explain at least two potential losses or quality changes. **Solution guidance:** reconcile consistent physical units and include process losses.\n\n**Question B \u2014 independent worked sensitivity.** Recalculate 402 kg CO\u2082e/tonne binder from an input of 36 %; calculate a third point midway between 36 and 70, using the formula rather than copying the interactive output. Explain why a difference of 238 kg CO\u2082e/tonne binder is not automatically a change in environmental benefit or service economics. **Solution guidance:** numerical sensitivity does not validate a site-specific benefit.\n\n**Question C \u2014 design a decisive controlled comparison.** For \u201cA contractor specifies a low-clinker mix for marine infrastructure but lacks durability evidence for chloride exposure.\u201d, provide a sample plan, at least one suitable control and one evidence source that does not share the same central assumption as the model. Describe the plausible observation that would invalidate the preferred conclusion. **Solution guidance:** compare equivalent service and independently qualified outputs.\n\n### 7. Decision memo and reader challenge\n\nWrite a memo that separates observations from synthetic assumptions and proposes a reversible pilot with explicit stop criteria. Maximizing substitution can compromise performance or depend on scarce byproducts that will decline as industries decarbonize.",
