          "content": "### 1. Physics, chemistry and the function actually supplied\n\nLithium, nickel, cobalt, graphite and rare-earth-related materials link engineered devices to mines, refining and waste streams. Extraction technologies and ore grades influence energy, water, tailings and occupational exposures. A tonne of ore cannot be equated with a tonne of useful element; concentration, recovery efficiency and refining yield matter. Material security and environmental burdens are related but distinct: a concentrated supply chain raises resilience risks even if its measured carbon intensity is low.\n\n**Working relationship:** Contained target mass = ore mass \u00d7 grade \u00d7 recovery fraction, with all units and losses defined. Keep the system boundary, physical units and quality specification visible before asserting an avoided impact.\n\n### 2. A real engineering dilemma with rival explanations\n\n**Problem statement:** A new battery cathode chemistry uses less cobalt but more nickel and manganese. Assess upstream trade-offs beyond mass totals. Propose two rival mechanisms and one observation that differentiates them. Record timing, composition and functional performance before ranking either explanation.\n\n**Constraint that can reverse the answer:** A high reported element recovery ignores tailings, local water stress and electricity supply. Estimate which service, quality or environmental condition could reverse the conclusion.\n\n### 3. Transparent synthetic calculation and sensitivity\n\nThe paired interactive card for this lesson is **Clinker fraction sensitivity**. Its computational expression is `1000*(0.85*p/100+0.15*(1-p/100))`, with adjustable **Clinker mass fraction of binder** in %. At 49 % the deterministic output is **493 kg CO\u2082e/tonne binder**; at 81 % it is **717 kg CO\u2082e/tonne binder**. The two-point sensitivity is 7 kg CO\u2082e/tonne binder per %. These are synthetic teaching calculations, not measurements. Independently verify units and a third input before interpretation.\n\n**Why the numerical output has limits:** 1000 kg binder, hypothetical clinker factor 0.85 kg CO\u2082e/kg and substitution factor 0.15; ignores durability changes. For a real decision, independently measure the variables that this illustrative formula holds constant.\n\n### 4. Evidence protocol, sampling and falsification\n\n**Specific investigation:** Request location-specific water, tailings, refining and verified human-rights due-diligence evidence. Define independent samples, record rejected outputs and capture instrument calibration and field-relevant service conditions.\n\n Verify both mass-balance closure and functionally qualified output; publish observations that contradict the preferred mechanism.\n\n### 5. Design trade-off and conditions of adoption\n\n**Trade-off:** Lower critical-element mass can reduce geopolitical exposure while shifting environmental harms to another extraction pathway. Evaluate the trade-off only after minimum safety, quality and legal requirements are satisfied.\n\n### 6. Three guided practice problems\n\n**Question A \u2014 reconstruct the physical account.** Starting from \u201cContained target mass = ore mass \u00d7 grade \u00d7 recovery fraction, with all units and losses defined.\u201d, identify the relevant system, quantities, physical units and hidden assumptions. Draw material or energy arrows, label the conserved quantity and explain at least two potential losses or quality changes. **Solution guidance:** reconcile consistent physical units and include process losses.\n\n**Question B \u2014 independent worked sensitivity.** Recalculate 493 kg CO\u2082e/tonne binder from an input of 49 %; calculate a third point midway between 49 and 81, using the formula rather than copying the interactive output. Explain why a difference of 224 kg CO\u2082e/tonne binder is not automatically a change in environmental benefit or service economics. **Solution guidance:** numerical sensitivity does not validate a site-specific benefit.\n\n**Question C \u2014 design a decisive controlled comparison.** For \u201cA new battery cathode chemistry uses less cobalt but more nickel and manganese. Assess upstream trade-offs beyond mass totals.\u201d, provide a sample plan, at least one suitable control and one evidence source that does not share the same central assumption as the model. Describe the plausible observation that would invalidate the preferred conclusion. **Solution guidance:** compare equivalent service and independently qualified outputs.\n\n### 7. Decision memo and reader challenge\n\nWrite a memo that separates observations from synthetic assumptions and proposes a reversible pilot with explicit stop criteria. Lower critical-element mass can reduce geopolitical exposure while shifting environmental harms to another extraction pathway.",
