          "content": "**Guiding question:** Can recovered critical materials supply credible new products without compromising safety?\n\nA producer takes back consumer electronics and battery packs. Cells differ in history and chemistry; the organization needs safe screening, traceability and a recovery route without claiming every collected device becomes new battery material. A product comparison only becomes meaningful after its required service, physical boundaries, time and mandatory safety specifications are written down. The system may contain long-lived inventories, poorly measured return streams, process losses, upstream impacts and financial constraints. The engineering objective is to find the particular intervention that actually improves useful output per resource and reduces meaningful harms. The unit combines an explicit material mechanism with a data and decision workflow, rather than presenting recycling, repair, substitution or efficiency as universally superior.\n\nAn evidence-ready project in this unit starts with **chemistry-specific, safety-reviewed recovered products**. Its indispensable records include **log origin, pack chemistry, sample purity, return rates and independent release testing**, and important counterevidence comes from **batch mass balances and qualified downstream acceptance certificates**. Before reading any calculated result, consider **whether high collection creates saleable materials at required safety level**. If the answer can be changed by a reasonable assumption about material quality or service life, the proper result is a bounded comparison and a plan to collect missing evidence, not a confident environmental winner.",
