          "content": "### 1. Physics, chemistry and the function actually supplied\n\nLithium-ion batteries span multiple cathode chemistries, anodes, solvents and pack architectures. Collection pathways must handle state-of-charge, physical damage, uncertain history and fire risk under qualified safety protocols. Black mass composition is a variable intermediate, not a standardized metal content. Recovered lithium, nickel and cobalt yields differ by technology; products must satisfy new manufacturing specifications before closed-loop claims are made.\n\n**Working relationship:** Recovered element mass = incoming active-material mass \u00d7 element fraction \u00d7 recovery yield for a specified process. Keep the system boundary, physical units and quality specification visible before asserting an avoided impact.\n\n### 2. A real engineering dilemma with rival explanations\n\n**Problem statement:** A recycler receives damaged battery modules from mixed vehicle fleets and consumer devices, with inconsistent records. Propose two rival mechanisms and one observation that differentiates them. Record timing, composition and functional performance before ranking either explanation.\n\n**Constraint that can reverse the answer:** Assuming constant composition and full metal recovery across product generations overstates output. Estimate which service, quality or environmental condition could reverse the conclusion.\n\n### 3. Transparent synthetic calculation and sensitivity\n\nThe paired interactive card for this lesson is **Recovered lithium in sorted battery stream**. Its computational expression is `60*p/100`, with adjustable **Element recovery yield** in %. At 36 % the deterministic output is **21.6 kg Li**; at 70 % it is **42 kg Li**. The two-point sensitivity is 0.6 kg Li per %. These are synthetic teaching calculations, not measurements. Independently verify units and a third input before interpretation.\n\n**Why the numerical output has limits:** 1000 kg active material at an illustrative fixed 6 mass% lithium; recovery and sorting ideal except variable yield. For a real decision, independently measure the variables that this illustrative formula holds constant.\n\n### 4. Evidence protocol, sampling and falsification\n\n**Specific investigation:** Document chemistry classes, qualified handling and independently assay all incoming and outgoing streams. Define independent samples, record rejected outputs and capture instrument calibration and field-relevant service conditions.\n\n Verify both mass-balance closure and functionally qualified output; publish observations that contradict the preferred mechanism.\n\n### 5. Design trade-off and conditions of adoption\n\n**Trade-off:** High-value selective recovery may be economical while lower-value chemistries demand different financing or policy arrangements. Evaluate the trade-off only after minimum safety, quality and legal requirements are satisfied.\n\n### 6. Three guided practice problems\n\n**Question A \u2014 reconstruct the physical account.** Starting from \u201cRecovered element mass = incoming active-material mass \u00d7 element fraction \u00d7 recovery yield for a specified process.\u201d, identify the relevant system, quantities, physical units and hidden assumptions. Draw material or energy arrows, label the conserved quantity and explain at least two potential losses or quality changes. **Solution guidance:** reconcile consistent physical units and include process losses.\n\n**Question B \u2014 independent worked sensitivity.** Recalculate 21.6 kg Li from an input of 36 %; calculate a third point midway between 36 and 70, using the formula rather than copying the interactive output. Explain why a difference of 20.4 kg Li is not automatically a change in environmental benefit or service economics. **Solution guidance:** numerical sensitivity does not validate a site-specific benefit.\n\n**Question C \u2014 design a decisive controlled comparison.** For \u201cA recycler receives damaged battery modules from mixed vehicle fleets and consumer devices, with inconsistent records.\u201d, provide a sample plan, at least one suitable control and one evidence source that does not share the same central assumption as the model. Describe the plausible observation that would invalidate the preferred conclusion. **Solution guidance:** compare equivalent service and independently qualified outputs.\n\n### 7. Decision memo and reader challenge\n\nWrite a memo that separates observations from synthetic assumptions and proposes a reversible pilot with explicit stop criteria. High-value selective recovery may be economical while lower-value chemistries demand different financing or policy arrangements.",
