          "content": "**Guiding question:** Can cleaner processing reduce harm without merely exporting it to another location?\n\nA manufacturing business switches from solvent-intensive cleaning to a water-based process. It reports reduced direct emissions but must investigate energy, wastewater, worker exposure and upstream sourcing. A product comparison only becomes meaningful after its required service, physical boundaries, time and mandatory safety specifications are written down. The system may contain long-lived inventories, poorly measured return streams, process losses, upstream impacts and financial constraints. The engineering objective is to find the particular intervention that actually improves useful output per resource and reduces meaningful harms. The unit combines an explicit material mechanism with a data and decision workflow, rather than presenting recycling, repair, substitution or efficiency as universally superior.\n\nAn evidence-ready project in this unit starts with **site-specific metered resource and hazard baselines**. Its indispensable records include **record process heat, water balance, wastewater, exposure pathways and suppliers**, and important counterevidence comes from **pre/post operational data, workplace controls and third-party sampling**. Before reading any calculated result, consider **whether reduced on-site burdens are shifted to another facility or community**. If the answer can be changed by a reasonable assumption about material quality or service life, the proper result is a bounded comparison and a plan to collect missing evidence, not a confident environmental winner.",
